// The record has said who each view is for since the storm was written.
//
//     read-model "what-is-ready-to-pick-up"  needed-by="working-agent"
//     read-model "the-published-set…"        needed-by="reader" "adopter"
//     read-model "capabilities-and-what…"    needed-by="maintainer"
//
// The published directory ignores every one of them. Seven files, alphabetical, no index —
// so a reader who wants outcomes opens `architecture/` because it sorts first, and a reader
// who wants the plumbing gets there by luck.
//
// The root cause is an ordering error in how these documents were built: what the record
// could COMPUTE was published, and who it was for was asked afterwards. That is user-centred
// design run backwards, and it produced six documents that are individually correct and
// collectively unnavigable.

thin-slice "TS.260821.14" {
    slug "a-front-door-per-reader"
    title "Publish one product story, and leave the engineering in the record"
    kind "view"
    realizes "CAP.document-projection"
    from-storm "ES.260819.01"

    read-model "where-to-start"
    actor "somebody opening a release directory for the first time, with a reason"
    needs-read-model "what-is-currently-true"

    trigger "a reader opens a release and finds seven sibling directories, four of them about how the tool is built"
    outcome "a release is one document read front to back, and the engineering views stay in the record where the CLI answers them"

    scenario #"""
        Given a record whose read models each declare `needed-by`
        When the set is published for a version
        Then the directory opens with an index grouped by those audiences, each entry naming the
             question that document answers — because a reader arrives with a reason, and seven
             siblings in alphabetical order ask them to already know the answer in order to find it.
        """#

    step "1" does="a release becomes ONE document with an order: WHY this exists, what it can do, HOW to start, what changed, what is known missing"
    step "2" does="the engineering views stop being publishable. decisions, architecture and the shipped-doctrine inventory are declared `working` — they live in the record, and `praxis` answers them"
    step "3" does="the ordering is a property of the document, not of a directory listing. A reader who stops after the first section has the answer they came for"
    step "4" does="the glossary carries only the words the story uses, in dependency order. The full vocabulary is `praxis schema --print`, for whoever wants it"
    step "5" does="`needed-by` decides what belongs in the story at all — a view no persona reads is not published, it is queried"

    excludes "removing the engineering views. They stop being PUBLISHED, which is a lifetime \
        decision and nothing else — `declare-a-lifetime` already owns it. A decision record is \
        not less true for being in the record rather than in a release directory"
    excludes "deciding what an audience MEANS. `reader`, `adopter` and `maintainer` are the \
        record's words; mapping them onto `consumer` and `engineer` would be the engine encoding \
        a vocabulary no record handed it, and would be wrong for any project whose audiences differ"
    excludes "writing a document per persona. The views exist and are declared; this orders them. \
        Producing a consumer edition and an engineer edition of the same facts is two copies, and \
        the second one goes stale"
    excludes "tiering the glossary by audience. Which words a consumer needs is a real question \
        this does not answer — ordering by dependency helps and does not solve it, and pretending \
        otherwise would close a finding that should stay open"

    layer "doctrine"    reaches="declaring a read model includes declaring who needs it, and that declaration now decides where it lands"
    layer "enforcement" reaches="a publishable view with no `needed-by` is reported, because a document nobody is for is one nobody will read"
    layer "docs"        reaches="the release directory answers `where do I start` before it answers anything else"

    claim "C1" text="a release is one document with a declared order, not a set of sibling directories" \
        settled-by="a test asserting the published set for a version is a single reader-facing document, and that its sections appear in the order the record declares rather than alphabetically"
    claim "C2" text="each entry carries the question that document answers, from the view's own `answers`" \
        settled-by="a test asserting the index's text for a view equals that view's declared `answers`, verbatim"
    claim "C3" text="a publishable view naming no audience is reported, not hidden" \
        settled-by="a test declaring a publishable view with no `needed-by`, asserting it is named in the index as unaddressed and reported by the checker"
    claim "C4" text="the glossary carries the words the story uses, in dependency order" \
        settled-by="a test asserting `frame` precedes `thin-slice` precedes `iteration`, and that a kind the story never mentions is absent — the full vocabulary is a command, not a release artifact"
    claim "C5" text="the engineering views are in the record and answerable there, not published" \
        settled-by="a test asserting decisions, architecture and the doctrine inventory are not in the published set, and that each is reachable from the corpus — publishing them was a lifetime decision and this reverses it"

    depends-on "TS.260821.12 — the documents exist and are worth ordering"
    depends-on "TS.260821.16 — the story opens with why, and the record has to hold one"

    useful-alone "a reader opening a release directory is told which file is theirs and what it \
        answers. Today they are told nothing, and the first thing they see is whichever folder \
        sorts first"
    useful-to "the-reader-of-a-release"

    attacks "S6"

    cut-from "the maintainer reading the published set as two personas — a consumer who wants \
        outcomes and an engineer who wants the plumbing" \
        note="the personas were the prompt; the finding is that the record had already answered \
            the question. `needed-by` has been on every read model since ES.260819.01 and no \
            projection has ever read it"

    note "amended before any of it was built. The first cut of this slice added an INDEX over \
        seven directories, which is the wrong fix if there should not be seven directories. A \
        release is a logical flow of product information; engineering and decisions belong in the \
        record, where whoever has the repository can ask the CLI for what is true NOW rather than \
        what was frozen at a version"

    principle-note "six documents individually correct and collectively unnavigable is what \
        happens when derivability decides what gets built. Every one of them answers a real \
        question; nothing decided which reader arrives with which question, because the record \
        made all six equally computable and the format made all six equally a table"

    proves "a reader is told where to start, in the record's own words about who they are"
    does-not-prove "that each document is right for the audience it names. `needed-by` says who \
        a view is FOR; whether the document serves them is what a walkthrough with a real reader \
        would say, and nobody has done one"
}
